- net
- pass

' This is the door of a world. A session is a secret that the person at the
' door keeps and shows. The world keeps only the hash of that secret.
'
' A session opens the door when the world keeps a grant for it. The grant is
' the hash and the time when the session stops. The custody that the world's
' own card states signs the grant. Thus the key that only runs a world opens
' no door. And a grant made before custody moved opens no door after the move.
'
' These are the rules, and they are pure. The foot signs the grant at its desk
' (desk.foil). The code that parses a request hashes the secret, and the
' secret goes no further (http_foot/session).
+ door
  + named
    ' This function gives the name that a session has: the hash of its secret.
    \ secret=str
    ^ nat
    = bytes (str/to_atom secret)
    | Sha256 (add bytes (bex (mul 8 secret.len)))
  + at
    ' This function gives the place where the world keeps the grant of a
    ' session.
    \ self=iota session=nat
    ^ path
    [self 'io 'door ($x session)]
  + grant
    ' This function gives a grant as the record that keeps it.
    \ g=pass/grant
    ^ myth
    | (myth/sing ['until] (pails/n g.until)).put(['sig] (pails/n g.sig))
  + open
    ' This function tells if a session opens the door of this world now.
    \ session=nat now=nat state=namespace
    ^ bool
    = self state.self
      me (quip_val self)
      home=path [self 'io 'mesa]
      custody (net/held ['custody] home state)
    ? (eq 0 session) .false
    ? (eq 0 custody) .false
    ?> (./some record) (sovereign_care .x (door/at self session) state).fil .false
    = g (pass/grant me session record.nat_at(['until] 0) record.nat_at(['sig] 0))
      stands (pass/standing 0 0 0 0 0 custody)
    | either/is_ok (pass/allowed me stands now g)
